mode: "100644"
text: "# Voice\n\n\"Brand voice\" is where most profiles go generic. \"Professional but approachable\" describes\nroughly every company on earth and tells a downstream skill nothing. This reference makes\nvoice **concrete and reusable** so any skill can write on-voice without guessing.\n\n**Boundary:** this reference captures voice **by interview**. If the user has a body of\nexisting writing (posts, emails, docs), hand off to `voice-builder` to derive the voice from\nthat corpus, then merge the result here. The profile *stores* the voice; `voice-builder`\n*derives* it; `writing-style-and-tone` *applies* it per piece and per moment.\n\nVoice has four capturable parts: **dimensions** (the personality), **lexicon** (the words),\n**signature devices** (the mechanics), and **context shifts** (how it flexes by platform).\n\n---\n\n## 1. Dimensions — place the brand on each spectrum\n\nDon't ask \"what's your voice?\" Ask the user to place the brand on each spectrum below, and to\nsay *where it shouldn't go*. Record a value plus a one-line note. These six cover almost every\nreal brand:\n\n1. **Formality** — Casual ‹———› Formal\n2. **Warmth** — Matter-of-fact ‹———› Warm & personal\n3. **Authority** — Peer / \"one of us\" ‹———› Expert / authoritative\n4. **Energy** — Calm & measured ‹———› Bold & high-energy\n5. **Humor** — Serious throughout ‹———› Playful & funny\n6. **Polish** — Plainspoken & raw ‹———› Polished & crafted\n\nTwo techniques that get honest answers:\n\n- **The two-brand test.** \"Name a brand whose social you'd love to sound like, and one you'd\n  hate to sound like.\" The contrast locates the brand faster than any scale.\n- **The \"we sound ___, never ___\" sentence.** Force one for each dimension that matters:\n  \"We sound confident, never arrogant.\" \"Warm, never cutesy.\" These pairs are gold for\n  downstream skills because they encode the *edge* — the failure mode to avoid.\n\n> A point on a slider is only useful with its guardrail. \"Funny\" without \"never at a\n> customer's expense\" produces posts that get screenshotted for the wrong reasons.\n\n---\n\n## 2. Lexicon — the actual words\n\nThis is the single most useful thing you can capture. Collect:\n\n- **Words & phrases we use** — the brand's natural vocabulary, signature phrases, how it\n  refers to its product/customers/category. (e.g., \"members\" not \"users\"; \"ship\" not\n  \"release\".)\n- **Words & phrases we ban** — jargon, clichés, hype words, anything off-brand. Almost every\n  brand should ban a few: \"synergy,\" \"game-changer,\" \"revolutionary,\" \"unlock,\" \"in today's\n  fast-paced world,\" \"we're thrilled to announce,\" \"🚀 to the moon.\" Ask which make them wince.\n  For the 2026 AI-tell vocabulary (the delve-class words that read as machine-written), don't\n  duplicate a list here — apply `writing-style-and-tone`'s tell cluster at edit time.\n- **How we say the hard things** — how the brand handles a price increase, an outage, a\n  competitor, a mistake. Tone under pressure reveals real voice.\n- **Spelling/style choices** — title case vs sentence case, Oxford comma, em-dash habits,\n  numerals vs words, regional spelling.\n\n---\n\n## 3. Signature devices — the mechanics that make it recognizable\n\n- **Hooks/openers** — does it open with a question, a bold claim, a number, a story? Any\n  recurring pattern.\n- **Sentence rhythm** — short and punchy? long and flowing? a mix? One-line paragraphs?\n- **Emoji policy** — none / sparingly / liberally, and which ones are on-brand (and which are\n  banned).\n- **Hashtag policy** — none / a few branded / many. Where they go.\n- **Punctuation tics** — em-dashes, ellipses, ALL-CAPS for emphasis, lowercase-everything.\n- **Formatting** — line breaks, lists, the \"hook → space → body\" structure on LinkedIn, etc.\n\n---\n\n## 4. Context shifts — voice flexes by platform\n\nVoice is consistent; register changes. Capture the deltas so skills don't write LinkedIn\nposts in TikTok's voice:\n\n- **LinkedIn / X-thoughtful:** usually a notch more formal, more authority.\n- **TikTok / Reels / Shorts:** faster, looser, more playful, sound-on energy.\n- **Instagram:** visual-led, warmer, lifestyle register.\n- **Threads / X-casual:** conversational, reactive, opinionated.\n- **Reddit / community (advisory only — not a WoopSocial publishing platform):** drop the\n  marketing voice entirely; be a helpful peer or don't post.\n\nRecord only the shifts that matter for the platforms this brand actually uses.\n\n---\n\n## How voice differs by business type (quick guidance)\n\n- **B2B SaaS:** authority + plainspoken usually wins; ban hype; let a strong POV carry\n  personality. A named human (founder) voice often outperforms the logo voice — 2026 trend\n  research (Hootsuite's Social Media Trends report) finds audiences trust employees and\n  founders more than faceless brand accounts (attribute; verify-quarterly).\n- **Ecommerce / DTC:** warmth + energy; product-as-hero; voice often playful and benefit-led.\n- **Local / SMB:** the owner's real human voice; community and place; low polish is an asset.\n- **Personal brand / creator:** maximum specificity — it's literally one person. Capture their\n  actual speech patterns and boundaries.\n- **Professional services (legal, finance, health):** authority + warmth, tightly bounded by\n  compliance; \"trustworthy, never stiff\" is a common target.\n- **Nonprofit:** warmth + clarity; dignity over pity; concrete impact over abstraction.\n\n---\n\n## The \"show, don't tell\" capture (do this for every brand)\n\nAdjectives drift; examples don't. Collect:\n\n- **3–5 posts the brand loves** (theirs or others'), each with one line: *what* they love\n  about it.\n- **1–2 posts they hate**, with *why*.\n\nStore these in the profile. Downstream skills pattern-match against real examples far more\nreliably than against a slider value. If the brand has no posts yet, use admired accounts as\nreferences and label them as aspirational.\n"
